---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import FasTriggerAnimation from '../../components/FasTriggerAnimation.astro';
// Review work locally without publishing an unfinished article or sitemap entry.
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return import.meta.env.DEV ? [{ params: { demo: 'fas-trigger' } }] : [];
}
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title="FAS trigger animation preview | Airgun Craft" description="Local working draft of an annotated FAS 604 trigger sequence." />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <Header />
    <main>
      <FasTriggerAnimation />
      <details>
        <summary>Reference notes for the third article</summary>
        <p>The supplied FAS 604 drawing is the visual reference. It shows component locations, not a measured motion sequence. The four new FAS 6004 photos show the external trigger, not internal sear movement.</p>
        <p><a href="https://www.airgunbbs.com/showthread.php?747719-Chiappa-FAS-6004">Iain K D’s firsthand FAS 6004 description</a> identifies the trigger–sear–hammer arrangement. The <a href="https://www.pyramydair.com/airgun-resources/manuals/FAS-6004-Air-Pistol-Manual.pdf#page=5">Chiappa manual</a> documents the trigger controls but does not verify the internal travel animated here.</p>
        <p>The full linkage motion and any differences between the 604 and 6004 need confirmation before treating this as an exact mechanical animation.</p>
      </details>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
<style>
  main { width:1000px; max-width:calc(100% - 2rem); box-sizing:border-box; padding:1rem 0 3rem; }
  details { margin-top:1.5rem; font-size:.85rem; }
  summary { cursor:pointer; }
</style>
